\section*{All-pairs diamond distortion in tree-segment Banach spaces}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 331; 2026-09-27), the asserted result is that countably branching depth-$k$ diamonds incur $D^2\ge1+k/4$ in the selected segment spaces, with further power-type bounds for path and recursive norms.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
On a countably branching tree, give finitely supported arrays the disjoint-segment norm
\[
 \rho(a)=\sup_{S_1,\ldots,S_m}\Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^m|\sum_{s\in S_j}a_s|^2\Bigr)^{1/2},
\]
where segments are ancestral intervals and are pairwise disjoint. Complete this norm on the forest $\coprod_{h\ge1}\mathbb N^{\le h}$ to obtain $J$ and set $X=J^*$. On $\mathbb N^{<\omega}$ obtain $J_{\infty}$ in the same way, and let $B_{\infty}$ be the closed span of coordinate evaluations in $J_{\infty}^*$. Form $\mathcal D_k$ by replacing each edge by countably many two-edge paths, halving the edge lengths at each stage. The claimed bound for every bi-Lipschitz embedding into either target is
\[
 D^2\ge1+k/4\quad(k\ge0),
\]
where $D$ is the ratio of upper and lower Lipschitz constants with respect to shortest-path distance.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 331; 2026-09-27), graph slopes select fresh coordinate windows along successive diamond levels. Atomic endpoint predictions and primal descent tests charge each layer's energy, so summation forces distortion growth; path costs give analogous estimates in the other norms.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 331; 2026-09-27).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 331; 2026-09-27), the documented Lean scope is the unconditional estimate $D^2\ge1+k/4$ for depth-$k$ diamonds in the two specified segment spaces; further graph deductions in the selected statements assume twenty-two companion inputs.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{BoundedTreePotentials.lean}, \texttt{DaugavetModuli.lean}, \texttt{DiamondDistortion.lean}, \texttt{ForestSpace.lean}, \texttt{IndependentProducts.lean}, \texttt{MidpointLenses.lean}, \texttt{RecursivePotentials.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.BoundedTreePotentials.TreeCalculus.main\_counterexample}, \texttt{OAI.ComparatorModel.RecursivePotentials.main}, \texttt{OAI.DiamondDistortion.headline\_all\_pairs}, \texttt{OAI.ExactModuli.exact\_example}, \texttt{OAI.ExactModuli.exact\_main}, \texttt{OAI.ExactModuli.kadets\_werner\_existence}, \texttt{OAI.ForestSpace.main\_theorem}, \texttt{OAI.IndependentProducts.main\_exponential}, \texttt{OAI.IndependentProducts.main\_gaussian\_12}, \texttt{OAI.IndependentProducts.main\_gaussian\_24}, \texttt{OAI.IndependentProducts.main\_general}, \texttt{OAI.SegmentLenses.midpoint\_lens\_main}.

